The inter-site path is healthy end-to-end. From pc1 (195.11.14.2) to the partner pc2 (200.1.1.2), ICMP tests succeed with 0% loss and ~0.5–1.6 ms RTT (confirmed by topology-wide reachability and direct ping). Reverse direction from pc2 to pc1 also succeeds with 0% loss. Routing control plane is up: on router1 and router2, FRR is active (systemctl shows running), both routers have the interconnect 193.10.11.0/24 interfaces up, and BGP has learned and installed the remote LANs (router1 shows BGP route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0; router2 shows BGP route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 on eth0). Kernel routing tables reflect these paths, and both host defaults point at their local routers correctly.

Mechanism-wise, if the “line between buildings” were down, we would see DOWN interfaces on router1:eth0 and/or router2:eth0, loss of BGP-learned routes, and failed pings; none of these conditions are present. Localization: the inter-router link router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0 is UP, and host-facing links router1:eth1 and router2:eth1 are also UP.

Root cause: No persistent network fault is present; the reported outage was transient or outside the network path (e.g., endpoint/application issue).

Corrective fix: No changes required. Ask the user to retry the order exchange now; if issues persist, investigate application/service logs on the endpoints and DNS. We also recommend monitoring router1/router2 interface and BGP logs for flaps to catch any intermittent future events.
